    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d385">

                <pb facs="0535" n="525" xml:id="pg525"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/8–2145</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Director of the Office of Far Eastern
                        Affairs</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Ballantine</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1945-08-28">August 28, 1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>I telephoned Ambassador Wei Tao Ming and said that we think that in view of the
                    publication of the text of the Sino-Soviet treaties<anchor n="58"
                        xml:id="d385-f1"/> there is no longer any need for the issuance of the
                    proposed statement in regard to China and we propose, subject to their
                    concurrence, to inform the British and Soviet Governments to that effect.</p>
                <p>He said that he had been talking with Mr. Soong<anchor n="59" xml:id="d385-f2"/>
                    and he fully agrees that there is no longer any need for publication of the
                    statement, especially as Mao Tze-tung, the Communist representative, has now
                    returned to Chungking.</p>
                <p>I also telephoned to Mr. Downey, Special Assistant to the Secretary of the Navy,
                    and referred to his memorandum to Mr. Dunn of August 22,<anchor n="60"
                        xml:id="d385-f3"/> and told him of our decision. He said that he thought it
                    very sensible and he felt sure that Secretary Forrestal<anchor n="60a"
                        xml:id="d385-f4"/> would concur.</p>
                <p>I also telephoned in the foregoing sense to Colonel Gerhard in the office of
                    Assistant Secretary McCloy.<anchor n="61" xml:id="d385-f5"/>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>J[<hi rendition="#small-caps">oseph</hi>] W. B[<hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">allantine</hi>]</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d385-f1" n="58" xml:id="d385-f1-content">
                <p>Signed at Moscow, August 14, Department of State, <hi rendition="#italic">United
                        States Relations With China</hi> (Washington, Government Printing Office,
                    1949), p. 585.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d385-f2" n="59" xml:id="d385-f2-content">
                <p>T. V. Soong, President of the Chinese Executive Yuan (Premier).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d385-f3" n="60" xml:id="d385-f3-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d385-f4" n="60a" xml:id="d385-f4-content">
                <p>James Forrestal, Secretary of the Navy.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d385-f5" n="61" xml:id="d385-f5-content">
                <p>Col. Harrison A. Gerhardt was Executive Officer in the Office of the Assistant
                    Secretary of War (McCloy).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
